Language data inside the engine
-------------------------------

Not everything ReLoq C speaks with is its authors' own work, and ReLoq C says
so itself. By its NOTICE and the NOTICE of the openEVV copy it vendors, parts
of its language rules and data are transcribed from IBM's Embedded ViaVoice
objects and from the ETI Eloquence 6.1 language modules Apple ships with its
operating systems. No licence in this repository or in ReLoq C covers that
data, and nobody here can license it to anyone. In openEVV's words:

    That data is IBM's work, not ours. The MIT licence does not cover it and
    we are in no position to license it to anyone.

    [...]

    If you mean to do more with this than read it and run it, the rights in
    that data are yours to sort out with whoever holds them, and who that is
    today is not simple. IBM published these objects. The engine underneath
    them was Eloquence, by Eloquent Technology, which passed to SpeechWorks,
    then ScanSoft, then Nuance, whose text-to-speech rights went to Cerence
    in the 2019 spinoff and are now the subject of Cerence Inc. v. Microsoft
    Corporation and Nuance Communications, Inc., D. Del. 1:25-cv-00553,
    filed 6 May 2025. None of this is legal advice.

That data is compiled into every build of the apps. It is not in this
repository.

